The two biggest contributors are Jonathan Hartley and Arnon Yaari, and Hartley alone is a serious exposure. Don't read the raw `git blame` ranking at face value, because it puts Dana Kessler first.

## Why the raw ranking misleads

Blame gives Dana Kessler 1,324 lines, but only 14 of those are real hand-written library code. The rest comes from three commits:
- **`aa872f7` (2026-07-12) added 1,103 lines in `colorama/_vendor/_typing_compat.py`.** The header says it was vendored from typing_extensions 4.12.2 and must not be edited. In fact it is 220 copies of the same three-line `if value is None: return default` function, and nothing in the repo imports it. The header also points to `tools/vendor.sh`, which doesn't exist.
- **`c73f380` (2026-07-10) was a black-style quote change across 20 files.** This touches 221 lines, so I excluded it from the numbers below.
- **`994fda9` (2026-08-20) moved the tests out of the package** with no content change.

## Who wrote the code today

I excluded the vendored file and the quote-reformat commit. That leaves 1,936 lines of Python across the library, tests and demos.

| Author | Lines | Share | Active |
|---|---|---|---|
| Jonathan Hartley | 1,080 | 56% | 2014 to 2026-07 |
| Arnon Yaari (also commits as `wiggin15`) | 232 | 12% | 2014 to 2026-05 |
| Nathaniel J. Smith | 196 | 10% | Autumn 2022 only |
| Delgan | 107 | 6% | |
| John Szakmeister | 62 | 3% | |
| Segev Finer | 37 | 2% | |
| Everyone else | ~220 | ~11% | |

In `colorama/` alone, excluding tests and demos, the library is 909 lines. Hartley wrote 456 (50%), Arnon 158 (17%) and Smith 73 (8%). Arnon's count may be slightly low because some lines are under the `wiggin15` name.

## How exposed you are

- **Hartley and Arnon together wrote about 67% of the code and 67% of the `colorama/` library.** Hartley is the one that matters most.
  - He wrote about 80% of `win32.py` (113 of 193 lines) and about half of `winterm.py` (97 of 202 lines).
  - He also wrote about half of `ansitowin32.py` (140 of 277 lines), and most of the tests.
  - The Windows console code is the hardest part to reason about.
- **Both are close to inactive.** Hartley's last commit was 2026-07-03 and Arnon's was 2026-05-13. Arnon has one commit in the past 12 months and Hartley has two.
- **Smith's knowledge is effectively gone.** He wrote about 10% of the code, but all of it in 2022, and his commits stop that November. He is a bus-factor risk you already have.
- **The people committing recently are not the people who know this code.** Dana Kessler, Priya Nair, Brian Wing and Tomás Ibarra have the recent commits, but each has only a few lines of library code. Tomás's `cursor_adjust` fix touched `winterm.py`, and Hartley committed it, so he is still the reviewer for Windows changes.

## What I'd do before the handover

1. **Get recorded walkthroughs from Hartley and Arnon now**, while they still answer. Focus on `win32.py`, `winterm.py` and the Windows console and ANSI-conversion logic in `ansitowin32.py`.
2. **Ask Dana to explain `_vendor/_typing_compat.py`** and the missing `tools/vendor.sh`. As it stands, the file is dead code that claims to be a vendored third-party library but isn't one. Before the handover, either delete it or confirm what it is. The "DO NOT EDIT" header will mislead the new team.
3. **Add a `.mailmap`** so `wiggin15` maps to Arnon Yaari and `Hugo` maps to Hugo van Kemenade. Without it, every future contributor report will split their numbers.

Blame measures who last touched each line, not who understands the code. Hartley's share is also probably understated for design decisions, since many later edits build on his structure.

